[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    61[0m:[38;5;10m[49m artifact:
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    62[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    63[0m:[38;5;10m[49m * **Symptom.** Calling the export with `256` (a promoted `int`, low 8 bits `0`)
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    64[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   gave, from the C `.so`, exactly the `c == 0` output (`control: 2`, everything
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    65[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   else `0`). The Rust `.so` instead printed `alphabetic: 1024`, `digit: 2048`,
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    66[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   `space: 8192`, `printing: 16384` and `to lower: 0x80` — values read from
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    67[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   past the end of the ctype table.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    68[0m:[38;5;10m[49m * **Cause.** The export was declared `extern "C" fn driver(c: c_char)`. rustc
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    69[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   tags an `i8` C-ABI parameter `signext`, so LLVM assumed the register already
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    70[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   held a sign-extended `char`, kept the full `256`, proved `c` was in
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    71[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   `-128..=127`, elided the bounds check on `CTYPE_B[c + 128]`, and read index
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    72[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   `384` of a 384-element table. gcc's `char`-taking callee, by contrast, reads
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    73[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   only the low 8 bits of the argument register.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    74[0m:[38;5;10m[49m * **Fix.** The export now takes `c_int` and truncates with `arg as c_char`,
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    75[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   reproducing the C's truncation; `ctype_index` additionally masks with `0x1FF`
